---

## What I Learned

### The SOC Is Not One Person — It's a Team

A SOC operates in layers. Junior analysts handle 
first-level alert triage — the first eyes on every 
incoming event. Above them, senior analysts investigate 
deeper. At the top, security engineers build and 
maintain the infrastructure the whole team depends on.

Understanding this hierarchy changed how I think about 
my role. When I investigate an alert in Wazuh, I am 
doing exactly what a Tier 1 analyst does — triaging, 
classifying, and deciding what needs escalation.

---

### Alerts Are Not Just Noise — They Tell A Story

One of the biggest shifts in my thinking came from 
understanding IOCs — Indicators of Compromise. An alert 
firing is not just a notification. It is evidence. 
Every alert contains:
- A source IP
- A timestamp
- A rule that was triggered
- A MITRE ATT&CK technique

When Suricata flagged Kali scanning my Ubuntu machine's 
database ports, I wasn't just seeing a Wazuh alert — 
I was reading the early stages of a potential attack.
LetsDefend taught me to read that story.

---

### The Tools That Power A SOC

**SIEM** — the central nervous system. Collects logs 
from every endpoint and surface, correlates them, and 
generates alerts. I built this with Wazuh.

**EDR** — watches endpoint behaviour. Not just logs 
but processes, file changes, registry modifications. 
Catches what firewalls miss.

**SOAR** — the playbook engine. When an alert fires, 
SOAR automates the response — isolating a machine, 
blocking an IP, notifying the team. It turns manual 
SOC workflows into automated ones. My home lab doesn't 
have SOAR yet — but understanding it means I know 
exactly what to build next.

**Threat Intelligence Feed** — context for every alert. 
Is this IP known malicious? Has this file hash been 
seen before? I implemented this concept with VirusTotal 
API integration in my Wazuh setup.

